WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.866 --> 00:00:04.210
Many manufacturers develop products with stage-gate,

2
00:00:04.407 --> 00:00:07.600
worked out by Robert Cooper from the nineteen eighties.

3
00:00:08.612 --> 00:00:12.119
The work runs in stages: scoping, the business case,

4
00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:14.371
development, testing and launch.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.662
Between two stages sits a gate: a decision moment,

6
00:00:18.744 --> 00:00:21.612
where management chooses on agreed deliverables.

7
00:00:22.658 --> 00:00:27.116
That is a go/kill decision: continue, stop, hold, or redo a stage.

8
00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:31.272
For hardware that is sensible, not old-fashioned.

9
00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:35.000
The cost of change rises the further along you are.

10
00:00:36.015 --> 00:00:38.988
Changing a CAD drawing takes a few hours;

11
00:00:39.104 --> 00:00:43.481
changing a hardened steel mould can cost tens of thousands of euros.

12
00:00:44.505 --> 00:00:47.326
And a change after delivery can mean a recall.

13
00:00:48.335 --> 00:00:50.715
On top come long lead times for tooling,

14
00:00:50.843 --> 00:00:52.898
and requirements like CE marking:

15
00:00:53.072 --> 00:00:56.938
the European declaration that a product meets safety requirements.

16
00:00:58.228 --> 00:01:02.408
Still, the stages hold much uncertainty: does the customer want this,

17
00:01:02.535 --> 00:01:03.812
does the design work?

18
00:01:04.534 --> 00:01:06.217
That is where agile helps.

19
00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:12.698
Cooper described in 2016, and with Sommer in 2018,

20
00:01:12.837 --> 00:01:16.030
sprints inside the stages: Agile-Stage-Gate.

21
00:01:17.078 --> 00:01:19.075
Sprints, short daily meetings,

22
00:01:19.238 --> 00:01:22.431
demos and retrospectives sit inside the stages;

23
00:01:22.593 --> 00:01:25.171
the gates stay for big investment decisions.

24
00:01:26.306 --> 00:01:29.696
Cooper reports good first results in a handful of companies,

25
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.037
like faster time to market, but also struggles; the evidence is still limited.

26
00:01:36.373 --> 00:01:38.300
What is done after a hardware sprint?

27
00:01:39.086 --> 00:01:42.081
Rarely a shippable product: something to test or show.

28
00:01:43.126 --> 00:01:48.768
A CAD model you walk through with a customer, a 3D-printed model, a prototype test,

29
00:01:48.931 --> 00:01:51.090
or control software on a test rig.

30
00:01:52.121 --> 00:01:55.929
The question stays: what did we learn these two weeks that we didn't know?

31
00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.249
For a Lean consultant: a gate resembles a quality gate;

32
00:02:00.446 --> 00:02:03.697
the pitfall is a paperwork check: are the documents there,

33
00:02:03.848 --> 00:02:05.450
not: does the product work?

34
00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.449
Ask: what must be proven at the next gate, and which sprint delivers that evidence?

35
00:02:12.155 --> 00:02:15.730
If the answer is the design file, the gate is a formality.

36
00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:22.359
An example with illustrative amounts: a new pump housing, each time the same change,

37
00:02:22.487 --> 00:02:23.903
another flange size.

38
00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:28.828
In the CAD drawing: four hours at ninety euros an hour,

39
00:02:28.956 --> 00:02:31.057
three hundred and sixty euros.

40
00:02:32.066 --> 00:02:36.060
After the first prototype: a new printed or milled prototype,

41
00:02:36.245 --> 00:02:38.068
about three thousand euros.

42
00:02:39.089 --> 00:02:44.140
After the hardened mould: thirty-five thousand euros, and six weeks of delay.

43
00:02:45.122 --> 00:02:48.953
Development takes twenty-six weeks: thirteen two-week sprints,

44
00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:53.051
thirteen chances to find mistakes while changes cost hundreds,

45
00:02:53.214 --> 00:02:54.770
not tens of thousands.

46
00:02:56.102 --> 00:03:00.270
A long-lead item has a lead time so long it drives the whole schedule.

47
00:03:01.255 --> 00:03:05.040
An injection mould, a casting, a special motor, a chip.

48
00:03:05.781 --> 00:03:10.228
Eight to twenty weeks for a mould is not unusual; always ask the supplier.

49
00:03:11.228 --> 00:03:16.012
The start of series production is the SOP, often fixed by a trade fair,

50
00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.136
a customer contract or a season.

51
00:03:19.214 --> 00:03:22.256
Then backward planning: count back from the SOP.

52
00:03:22.995 --> 00:03:28.452
Minus validation, minus trial runs and corrections, minus building the tool,

53
00:03:28.661 --> 00:03:29.729
minus ordering.

54
00:03:30.748 --> 00:03:34.904
What is left is the last moment to fix the design: the design freeze.

55
00:03:35.723 --> 00:03:38.916
Then changes go through a formal engineering change.

56
00:03:40.231 --> 00:03:42.239
Here agile clashes with hardware.

57
00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:47.741
Sprints want to learn and adjust; long-lead items demand early decisions.

58
00:03:48.741 --> 00:03:52.769
So decide early on the long-lead items, keep the rest flexible,

59
00:03:52.909 --> 00:03:57.100
and fix the interfaces early, like mounting points and connections,

60
00:03:57.193 --> 00:03:59.956
so the inside of a module can still change.

61
00:04:01.015 --> 00:04:03.175
A second tool is set-based design:

62
00:04:03.372 --> 00:04:07.575
keep a few design options open and drop them only with enough knowledge,

63
00:04:07.831 --> 00:04:10.072
rather than repairing one early design.

64
00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:16.456
It comes from research on Toyota in the nineteen nineties, and SAFe names it too.

65
00:04:17.494 --> 00:04:21.894
In practice: cheap aluminium trial moulds for two variants first,

66
00:04:22.045 --> 00:04:25.087
soft tooling; then the hardened steel mould for the winner.

67
00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:30.046
Often purchasing is not at the sprint planning or the PI Planning.

68
00:04:31.051 --> 00:04:34.824
Purchasing hears about a mould when the design is almost done.

69
00:04:35.523 --> 00:04:37.624
By then the lead time is too long.

70
00:04:38.641 --> 00:04:42.426
Put long-lead items on the planning board as fixed milestones,

71
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.016
and invite purchasing.

72
00:04:45.045 --> 00:04:49.016
Ask in every hardware project: which part has the longest lead time,

73
00:04:49.201 --> 00:04:51.790
and by which week must its design be fixed?

74
00:04:53.152 --> 00:04:57.135
An e-bike maker wants series production to start in week forty;

75
00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.701
the battery housing is injection moulded.

76
00:05:00.718 --> 00:05:04.677
Back from week forty: validating the first series takes four weeks,

77
00:05:04.758 --> 00:05:06.801
so start by week thirty-six.

78
00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:10.802
Trial runs and mould corrections: three weeks,

79
00:05:10.907 --> 00:05:13.357
so the mould is ready in week thirty-three.

80
00:05:14.325 --> 00:05:18.981
Building the mould takes fourteen weeks by the quote: start in week nineteen.

81
00:05:19.671 --> 00:05:23.839
Ordering and approval take a week: design freeze by week eighteen.

82
00:05:24.841 --> 00:05:30.321
Check: forty minus four, minus three, minus fourteen, minus one, is eighteen.

83
00:05:31.071 --> 00:05:35.622
Until then the housing can improve; the rest of the bike stays flexible longer.
